      "knowledge_point_id": "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4",
      "knowledge_point": "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/b77fa9e3a398cf9f6cc3a3ea163b83cb75f1e5c9ea98aa1ae34730616c47977c.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
