    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-70303",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cbe80595-c807-506c-a356-566f1edb3402",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Less Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities",
      "problem": "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-2x+3<0.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-2x+3<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-2x+3\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2x+3 & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/289eed0e3b3047987dfbc729d0c22af32d1297ed2fb2d564ec725868b92a39c1.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-3$ or $x>1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
